+      "decision": "Accounts, organizations and ventures. A person holds a user account. An organization is the tenant: a lasting group of people with a shared purpose. A venture is an undertaking that organization owns. The glossary gives each word with the source it is taken from. The decisions after it are Torian's, settled 2026-11-02.\n\nGlossary\n\nVenture. An undertaking involving chance, risk or danger (Merriam-Webster, https://dictionary.findlaw.com/definition/venture.html). Shane and Venkataraman put the opportunity at the center of entrepreneurship, and Gartner treats the organization as something the new venture creates (https://effectuation.org/hubfs/Journal%20Articles/2017/06/The-Promise-of-Entrepreneurship-as-a-field-of-research.pdf, https://journals.aom.org/doi/10.5465/amr.1985.4279094). In Naive a venture is an undertaking to find, test and pursue one opportunity under uncertainty. It moves through Naive's phases and gates. An organization owns it. A sole founder's venture is owned by that person's one-person personal organization. Blank, Ries, and the W3C Organization Ontology's OrganizationalCollaboration call a startup or a venture a kind of organization (https://steveblank.com/2010/01/25/whats-a-startup-first-principles/, https://leanstartup.co/resources/articles/lean-startup-method/, https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/#org:OrganizationalCollaboration). This record follows the undertaking reading.\n\nOrganization. A collection of people organized together into a community or other social, commercial or political structure, with a common purpose that goes beyond the set of people, able to act as an agent (W3C Organization Ontology, https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/#org:Organization). The organization is the tenant. A tenant is the isolation boundary of a multi-tenant system, and each customer is one tenant (AWS SaaS Lens, https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/saas-lens/tenant.html, https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/saas-lens/tenant-isolation.html). WorkOS, Auth0 and Clerk map that tenant to the customer organization (https://workos.com/blog/model-your-b2b-saas-with-organizations, https://auth0.com/docs/manage-users/organizations/organizations-overview, https://clerk.com/docs/guides/organizations/overview). Memberships, roles, isolation and storage scoping are per organization. A row that belongs to a venture also carries the venture id.\n\nUser account. The sign-in identity of one person. GitHub: every person signs in to a user account, and an organization account cannot be signed into (https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/types-of-github-accounts). Only a person holds a user account. Stripe uses account for a business inside an organization (https://docs.stripe.com/get-started/account/orgs/setup). This record follows GitHub's user account.\n\nMembership. The link among an agent, an organization and a role, and it can carry the interval of membership (W3C Organization Ontology, https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/#org:Membership). One membership per person per organization. It holds the roles that person has there. A role can be granted across the organization or on one venture. The same ontology leaves role categories to a domain profile (https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/), which here is the framework's base roles plus roles the organization adds. An organization may itself be a member of an organization (W3C Organization Ontology, https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/).\n\nLegal entity. A person or organization with separate legal rights, such as owning property, entering contracts and paying taxes (Cornell LII Wex, https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/entity). The W3C FormalOrganization is an organization recognized in legal jurisdictions (https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/). In Naive a legal entity is a separate, optional record linked to an organization: an LLC, an EIN and the like. Many organizations never have one.\n\nPersonal organization. Each user account gets one, and the person is its only member. It holds their private material and their early ventures. Vercel turned a personal account into a one-person team that holds that person's projects (https://vercel.com/changelog/2024-01-account-changes). This is that shape.\n\nTransfer. A venture moves from one organization to another with its history, gates and judgments. The move does not copy the venture. Vercel transfers a project to another team the same way (https://vercel.com/docs/projects/transferring-projects). Every venture belongs to exactly one home organization at a time.\n\nJoint venture. Two or more parties that pursue one enterprise or project and share its risks (Cornell LII Wex, https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/joint_venture). A true joint venture forms a new shared organization whose members include the partner organizations. Their people still act through their own user accounts. Lighter collaboration uses guest access on the home organization.\n\nTeam. A group of organization members that reflects the group's structure (GitHub, https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/organizing-members-into-teams/about-teams). The W3C OrganizationalUnit is a department or support unit that is part of a larger organization and is not a legal entity of its own (https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/#org:OrganizationalUnit). A team is a group inside an organization.\n\nProject. A temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product, service or result (PMI, https://www.pmi.org/about/what-is-a-project). A project is bounded work inside a venture. schema.org makes Project a subtype of Organization (https://schema.org/Project). This record does not.\n\nWorkspace. A person's view of the organizations they belong to. It is never a stored record. Linear calls a workspace the home for all data in an organization, and Slack gives each workspace its own members and files (https://linear.app/docs/workspaces, https://docs.slack.dev/enterprise/). This record does not use workspace that way.\n\nBusiness. An activity or trade carried on with the intent of making a profit (Cornell LII Wex, https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/business). A label, not a record.\n\nCompany. A form of business organization that engages in commercial activity for profit, commonly a separate legal entity such as a corporation, a limited liability company, a partnership or a sole proprietorship (Cornell LII Wex, https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/company). A label, not a record.\n\nEnterprise. Reserved for a possible level above organizations, and not a record now. The word already means a company of establishments under common control (US Census Bureau, https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/susb/about/glossary.html), any legal entity or associated group (18 U.S.C. 1961, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/18/1961), and an undertaking (schema.org Project, above). The reserved sense is the admin tier in a GitHub enterprise account and a Slack Enterprise organization (https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/overview/about-enterprise-accounts, https://docs.slack.dev/enterprise/).\n\nDecisions\n\n1. Only a person holds a user account.\n2. A person joins an organization through one membership record for that person and that organization. The membership holds their roles there. A role can be granted across the organization or on one venture.\n3. The organization is the tenant. A member reaches its data only through their membership. Every venture belongs to exactly one home organization at a time.\n4. The framework ships base roles (governance/venture-template/roles/). An organization may add its own roles on top. The team plan already allows a role id that is not in the registry when the role has a name (schema/team-plan.schema.json, checked by schema/check-team.mjs). Those roles belong on the membership.\n5. Creating an account also creates a personal organization, and the person is its only member. It holds their private material and their early ventures. A sole founder's venture is owned by that personal organization. Founder is a role a person holds, on the organization or on one venture. It is not its own record type. This replaces the phrases founder suite and founder sandbox.\n6. Material leaves a personal organization for another organization only when the person shares specific items. The other organization receives a readable reference to those items. It does not receive a copy.\n7. When the person revokes that share, or leaves the organization, that organization's access to the shared items ends. Judgments it already recorded about them stay, including Jev scores and accepts.\n8. A workspace is a person's view of the organizations they belong to. It is never a stored record.\n9. An agent reaches an organization through a role there, granted across the organization or on one venture. It acts on behalf of a named person. It receives only the reads it declared. Declared reads are checked the same way writes are. Every function declares which parts of a record it reads and receives only those parts, checked the same way.\n10. All organizations are stored in one database. Every row and every query is scoped by organization id. A row that belongs to a venture also carries the venture id.\n11. A venture's code stays in its GitHub repositories, linked from the venture record. What syncs in is metadata only: branches, pull requests and checks. The knowledge base lives in the database, scoped by organization id: decisions, Discovery records, judgments and provenance. An agent writes code on a fresh worktree branch for each work item. That branch reaches main only through a gated pull request.\n\nA venture moves from one organization to another by transfer, with its history, gates and judgments. A true joint venture forms a new shared organization whose members include the partner organizations. Their people still act through their own user accounts. Lighter collaboration uses guest access. A legal entity is a separate, optional record linked to an organization. Business and company are labels, not records. Enterprise is reserved for a possible level above organizations.\n\nThe founder puzzle is a framework form, and any venture can run it. Naive's growth site is one user of that form. Puzzle answers belong to a venture inside the founder's personal organization. Naive keeps a reference to those answers and its own judgments (Jev scores, and accepts by Torian). The puzzle is the Discovery phase's intake form, and its answers become Discovery records. Minimal is not a property of a record. Each gate, at one specific point, defines what that gate requires. A gate reads only facts about an answer: accepted, consented, and by whom. A gate does not read the answer text.\n\nNot built yet. This record does not change code, schemas or earlier entries. What disagrees is listed under Conflicts and follow-ups. Moving storage into one database needs a migration plan and Torian's OK. This record does not propose running a migration.\n\nConflicts and follow-ups\n\n1. The venture folder is the tenant. schema/venture.schema.json describes one venture as anything a builder undertakes, from a hobby to a company (EE-011), with venture.json at the root of a folder that holds its products, its accounts and nested ventures. EE-011 says the venture is the unit of an instance and that ventures can hold ventures. interface/router.mjs resolves a venture to that folder, and knowledge/connectors/filesystem.mjs stores its records there. This record splits that unit. The organization is the tenant and the owner. The venture is the undertaking inside exactly one home organization. A company is a label, not that folder. A follow-up narrows the unit-of-an-instance and hobby-to-a-company sentences in EE-011. This pull request does not rewrite EE-011.\n\n2. Layout names. EE-086 and the layout description on schema/venture.schema.json put the knowledge base in knowledge/ and code in an optional repo/. schema/venture-paths.mjs names those folders k-base/ and codebase/ (KBASE and CODEBASE) and still reads a knowledge/ folder left by an earlier structured layout. schema/port-venture.mjs moves a brought-in folder into codebase/ and records it as a read-only filesystem source. Both layouts are folders inside the venture this record no longer treats as the tenant. The knowledge base belongs in the database, scoped by organization id, and the code belongs in GitHub, linked from the venture. Follow-up: choose folder names only if a migration plan still needs them. Do not migrate folders from this record.\n\n3. The workspace file is a stored record, under two names. schema/workspace.schema.json describes a file with a vault, a list of venture folders, and settings that govern what crosses between them (cross_venture_reads). schema/workspace.mjs loads naive-workspace.json, and loads workspace.json only when that file's schema field is everything-ecosystem. schema/founders.ts init writes the founder pointer onto workspace.json, and check reads workspace.json, so a folder that has only naive-workspace.json never receives that pointer. schema/data-model.json lists instance/workspace.json and schema/examples/workspace/workspace.json for the workspace record. docs/ontology.json and EE-002 say a workspace is declared by workspace.json. schema/workspace.mjs canRead allows a published artifact to cross when the target is listed in the workspace file and cross_venture_reads is on, with no share from a person. Follow-up: a workspace is a view derived from organization memberships, and it is never a stored record. Sharing is a reference a person makes.\n\n4. Three founder definitions, and none points at the others. EE-094, schema/founders.ts and schema/workspace-founder.schema.json (data-model id workspace-founder) describe a person of the workspace at founders/<handle>/founder.json, before any venture, with workspace.json pointing at that file. schema/founder.schema.json (data-model id founder) describes one founder of a venture at people/founders/<id>.json, with role founder, co-founder or general-partner. schema/terms/terms.ts defines founder as a membership role checked by properties F1 to F5, and docs/ontology.json cites that term on the noun founder appointment, not on either schema. The data model lists the two record types apart, and neither refs the other. schema/people.schema.json keeps everyone in one people/people.json, each person with a single role string, and its description points only at governance/venture-template/roles/venture-roles.json. There is no membership record, one per person per organization, and no way to grant a role on one venture separately from the organization. Follow-up: one membership holds the roles, founder among them, and the personal organization replaces founders/. Early ventures, including puzzle answers, live inside that organization. This entry supersedes the founder-record model in EE-094. EE-094's status field is still in-force, because this pull request does not rewrite earlier entries. A follow-up sets that status to superseded.\n\n5. Founder suite, founder sandbox, and minimal. EE-092 says a blank venture template lives in the founder suite and becomes a venture when it is a minimally viable venture. schema/founders.ts documents show() as the founder suite as a page. No record type, term id or path is named founder suite. The words founder sandbox do not occur in this repository. Follow-up: the personal organization holds private material and early ventures. Retire both phrases. Replace the minimally viable venture checklist with gates, each gate naming the facts it requires at its point. Minimal is not a field on a record.\n\n6. Reads are not declared and checked the way writes are. schema/access.mjs: a role the team plan does not list, or a role with no routes, may reach every route (globsOf returns null and may returns true). schema/team-plan.schema.json says the same about a missing routes object. interface/acting.mjs reach() returns read and write globs of ** in that case. The same file refuses an agent that acts in a role whose occupant is person, so an agent does not act on behalf of a named person. schema/visibility.mjs publicView omits fields that are not public, and interface/router.mjs applies that view only when the actor is public. Every other actor receives the merged record. mayKnow in schema/visibility.mjs can hide a whole confidential record from an actor who is not on its access list. It does not hide fields inside a record the actor may know. schema/gate.mjs evaluate, and interface/gate-hooks.mjs gateWrite, judge a write. A read does not pass through the gate. interface/kit-boundary.mjs checks route globs for a kit workflow only, and it allows a read when the route is listed under reads or under writes. Follow-up: every agent and every function declares the parts of a record it reads, receives only those parts, and that check runs the way the write check runs. A role with no declaration reaches nothing. An agent's role is on the organization, across it or on one venture, and the agent acts for a named person.\n\n7. Records are files in a venture folder. knowledge/connectors/filesystem.mjs is the default source. Provenance is provenance.jsonl (EE-031). platform/connectors/github.mjs reads and writes file contents in a GitHub repository, and a write opens a pull request against the branch in ref (default main). schema/venture.schema.json repos store a url and a location relative to the workspace code folder (EE-020). schema/port-venture.mjs copies code into the venture folder. Nothing stores every organization in one database with every row and every query scoped by organization id, and nothing adds a venture id on the rows that belong to a venture. Nothing syncs GitHub as branches, pull requests and checks only, linked from the venture. Nothing opens a fresh worktree branch per work item. Follow-up: write the migration plan and get Torian's OK before any migration. Do not run a migration from this record.\n\n8. The puzzle on pull request 160. On this branch, EE-094 says the founder puzzle never reaches a route or a model, and site/ has no puzzle page. Pull request 160, its EE-101 (2026-11-02), says the puzzle stays in founders/<handle>/_private/ and still never reaches a route or a release, and the same decision puts the conversation on the site at /founders/puzzle. On that branch, site/app/(site)/founders/puzzle/page.tsx renders the chat, and site/lib/founders/actions.ts calls openGranted and answerGranted, which read and write the puzzle record. That pull request's EE-104 stores the transcript at founders/<handle>/_private/puzzle.json, or in Neon when PUZZLE_DATABASE_URL is set, keyed by handle. Its EE-101 sends the answer text as the scoring payload. Follow-up: the puzzle is a framework form, Naive's growth site is one caller, and answers live in a venture inside the founder's personal organization. Naive stores a reference plus judgments. Gates read accepted, consented and by whom. EE-101 on pull request 155 is a different decision (language models write, Jev judges, code checks). EE-101 through EE-104 are already used by pull requests 155, 156, 157, 158 and 160, so this record is EE-105.\n\n9. Personal organization and EE-009. EE-009 says personal life is out of scope, there is no personal root, and personal knowledge management is a separate system. The personal organization is a one-person organization for the person's private material as a builder, and for the early ventures they have not transferred. Follow-up: record whether that narrows the no-personal-root sentence and leaves personal life out of scope. This pull request does not rewrite EE-009.\n\n10. User account and the party record. schema/account.schema.json (EE-021) is a named party a venture keeps, at accounts/<id>/account.json. Its description says an account follows CRM usage, an organization or a person, with relationships such as client, customer, user, member and partner. That party record is not the user account, which only a person holds. Follow-up: keep the party record as the party an organization serves, under a name that is not user account. A company is a label, not a holder of a user account and not a venture.",
